package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyPhyrexiaOrigins = StorySection(
    "phyrexia-origins", "Phyrexia's Origins",
    "Before Phyrexia was a threat it was a cure, a dead planeswalker's empty world that a Thran healer took for his own and filled with the sick who trusted him.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "halcyon", "Halcyon",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "eight-terraces", "Eight Terraces",
                    listOf(
                        "Halcyon was the capital of the Thran Empire in its last golden age, and it floated. Powerstone engines held the city in the air above an extinct volcano, its districts climbing through eight terraces toward a western plateau. Its streets were white brick winding between limestone townhouses roofed in blue tile, and on the highest terrace stood minarets and onion domes, flying arches, a great stadium, the Council Hall and the high court. A wide white wall ringed it all, and through its archways lay five aerial ports where merchant caravels hovered at anchor.",
                        "The Thran had come from the east and carved out an empire across two thirds of Terisiare, eight city-states joined by a single highway through the desert. Phoenon was the oldest. On the site of another, Nyoron, the city of [Kroog](the-war) would one day be built. The Thran governed themselves by votes, first by the shout of a full assembly, then by the eldest delegate of each city, and if those were split, by a council of leaders who had proven their worth to the empire.",
                        "Two of those leaders were married to each other. Glacian was Halcyon's chief artificer, the genius who had learned to trap mana inside crystal and so invented the powerstones on which everything the Thran built depended. He had even shown that the multiverse must exist by proving it was mathematically necessary. His wife Rebbec was the city's chief architect. The cards remember them as {Glacian, Powerstone Engineer} and {Rebbec, Architect of Ascension}, each carrying a line of praise from the other.",
                        "Beneath the floating city lay the price of all that light. The machine that made Halcyon's powerstones sat over a network of caves, and in those caves lived the people the Thran called Untouchables.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Glacian built his automatons to last, and last they did, beyond even the Thran themselves.",
                        "Thran Vigil",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "untouchables", "Untouchables",
                    listOf(
                        "The caves under Halcyon were called the Caves of the Damned. Thousands of years later they would be known as [Koilos](koilos). The Untouchables crowded into them lived directly beneath the mana rig that produced the city's powerstones, and they were sick. Their bodies rotted in spots and their minds failed. Thran healing magic made the sickness worse, not better.",
                        "The anger in the caves found a leader in a man named Gix. During a riot he got close to Glacian and stabbed him with a shard of powerstone. Glacian survived the wound, but a disease set in that no Thran healer could touch, and the more they treated him with magic the worse he grew.",
                        "Rebbec would not watch him die. She used her standing to make the reluctant Elder Council recall a man they had exiled five years before. He was a eugenicist, a champion of the faction that had lost the century-long civil war against the empire's artificer elite, and he treated the body as a marvelous machine to be studied and repaired. His name was [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth). He was thirty-five, tall and dark-haired and very sure of himself, and the people of Halcyon had no idea what he had done while he was away.",
                        "In exile he had travelled the world in pursuit of knowledge and left a trail of plagues behind him. He set a sickness called the Black Cough loose among the dwarves of Oryn Deeps and a white death among the minotaurs of Talruum, and he loosed a plague on the elves of [Argoth](argoth). He infected the rulers of the cat folk with rabies until they tore each other apart, and pithed and vivisected the ruler of the Shivan viashino to see how he worked. The card {Yawgmoth, Thran Physician} shows the man Rebbec let back through the gates.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "phthisis", "Phthisis",
                    listOf(
                        "Yawgmoth found the answer quickly. Glacian's disease was caused by long exposure to powerstone radiation, the very energy that powered the empire. He called it phthisis, a name later borrowed for the card {Phthisis}. The elite, many of whom seemed naturally resistant, did not want to believe it, and Glacian distrusted Yawgmoth from the start. But the Untouchables, living under the mana rig, were riddled with it. When word of the diagnosis reached the caves, Gix began rallying them for revenge.",
                        "Yawgmoth won funding and apprentices from the council, and from a healer named Xod he took the idea of a serum of metal powders, copper, silver, gold and iron, each of which turned one color of mana away from the patient. Sores shrank at once. The effect lasted about a week. What he told no one was that he had left out lead, so his patients went on soaking up black mana and were slowly corrupted by it.",
                        "When Gix led the Untouchables up into the city, Yawgmoth broke the rebellion by offering them his serum for free. For that he was given a seat on the council and power over public health. He used it like a weapon. He declared the disease contagious, which a planeswalker would later accuse him of knowing was false, and sent anyone who opposed him down into the caves as infected, while bringing healthy Untouchables up in their place. Later it emerged that he had been diluting the serum he sent to the caves, and the riots that followed won him more money and command of the Halcyte Guard.",
                        "At the last of those riots he was ready. With a device built from Glacian's own designs he seized control of every powerstone machine in the city, crushed the uprising and forced Gix into complete obedience. Gix swore to serve him forever, and kept that oath across the ages. Glacian, meanwhile, was getting worse.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-vote", "The Vote",
                    listOf(
                        "Glacian's sickness did not respond to the serum at all. It progressed. He grew paranoid and forgetful, splintered into different personalities, and began to see traitors everywhere, above all in the healer who had become one of the most powerful people in the empire. Yawgmoth had reasons of his own to want Glacian out of the way. He had fallen in love with Rebbec, and she with him, though neither acted on it. He had Glacian banished to the Caves of the Damned while Rebbec poured herself into building the Thran Temple, an enormous structure hovering above the city.",
                        "A planeswalker came to Halcyon, a woman named Dyfed, to meet the genius who had proven the multiverse existed. She also knew that Glacian carried a latent spark of his own, though she said nothing of it. Yawgmoth walked in on the meeting and talked her into helping him instead. She agreed to search the planes for an empty world where he could build a paradise for the Thran.",
                        "Then his past arrived at the gates. A delegation came to Halcyon from the peoples Yawgmoth had poisoned and butchered in exile, elves, dwarves, minotaurs, cat folk and viashino, joined by Thran city-states that resented his growing power. They demanded that the empire give him up or face war. The city-states voted and split four against four. The council of leaders voted and split evenly too, all the way down to its last two members. They were Rebbec and Yawgmoth, and both voted that he should stay.",
                        "Yawgmoth overthrew the council the moment the vote was done. He imprisoned the elders and the foreign delegates and declared martial law, and the Thran went to war with half the world on his behalf. That war, the [Thran Phyrexian War](thran-phyrexian-war), would end the empire.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Commander Legends"),
                StorySource("Set", "Modern Horizons"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Thran, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Halcyon"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Thran"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Yawgmoth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phthisis"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "thran-phyrexian-war", "Thran Phyrexian War",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dyfed", "Dyfed",
                    listOf(
                        "Dyfed kept her promise to [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth). Searching the multiverse, she found a plane unlike any she had seen, wholly artificial, two or three times the size of the Thran Empire, built as nine spheres nested one inside the other and crawling with mechanical life. Its creator, an ancient planeswalker, had died in the innermost sphere about a month before, and with no will to direct it the plane was beginning to decay. Dyfed suggested that Yawgmoth take control of it before it did.",
                        "With Dyfed's help, and with insights into powerstone physics drawn from the increasingly delusional Glacian, Yawgmoth opened a permanent portal between that plane and the caves below [Halcyon](halcyon). Dyfed cracked a powerstone to make it. Yawgmoth bound himself to the plane at its core and became, in effect, its god. He named it [Phyrexia](yawgmoths-spheres) and offered it to the Thran as a refuge from powerstone sickness, and Rebbec, still not seeing what he was, designed an infirmary for it.",
                        "Phthisis patients went through the portal and were implanted with empty powerstones that drained their sickness away. They began to change, growing longer, thinner, stronger and faster. Gix was among the first to give up his humanity entirely and rise as something new, and he became Yawgmoth's right hand.",
                        "Dyfed also carried the imprisoned elders of the Thran council away to the plane of [Mercadia](mercadia), where they would become the ancestors of the Mercadians and of the Kyren goblins who served them. And while Rebbec was not looking, Yawgmoth took the two halves of the powerstone Dyfed had cracked to make the portal and hid them in the old wound in Glacian's body, where their radiation would finish what Gix's knife had begun.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "megheddon-defile", "Megheddon Defile",
                    listOf(
                        "The war began well for Yawgmoth's enemies. The alliance against him counted some twenty-five nations of minotaurs, elves, dwarves, viashino and cat folk, the humans of Gulatto Meisha, and the Thran city-states that had turned on Halcyon. Orleason, which had voted for Yawgmoth, fell almost without a fight when its own artificers changed sides. Phoenon, the oldest city of the empire, fell after a short siege.",
                        "Yawgmoth's answer to Phoenon told everyone what kind of war this would be. He bombed the city, killing the invaders inside it and every one of its own people with them. The card {Fall of the Thran} remembers how the empire ended, and it ended like this, one city at a time.",
                        "At Megheddon Defile, a mountain pass across the desert from Halcyon, the alliance army met his Halcyte Guard and the soldiers he had remade in Phyrexia, and was torn apart. Only its artifact creatures could stand against them. Then Yawgmoth seized the Null Sphere, a vast Thran station for broadcasting commands to artifacts, and turned every one of those machines against its masters. He finished the battle with a stonecharger, a weapon that left a killing cloud behind it, and obliterated the defile and everyone in it.",
                        "Minutes after the battle ended, two more alliance armies appeared on the horizon, marching on Halcyon itself. Yawgmoth meant to drop more stonechargers on them and use the Null Sphere to keep the cloud away from his own city. Before the siege closed in, he went down into Phyrexia, and Rebbec followed him there.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "rebbec-in-phyrexia", "Rebbec in Phyrexia",
                    listOf(
                        "In Phyrexia Yawgmoth merged himself with Rebbec and offered her the plane to rule beside him. She refused. He was furious, but Halcyon was under siege and he had to go back. The merger left its mark, though. Phyrexia now recognized Rebbec as Yawgmoth's equal, and it let her go wherever she liked.",
                        "On the upper spheres Yawgmoth had been curing and improving people. Lower down, Rebbec found the foreign delegates he had imprisoned, mutilated. She found Dyfed. The planeswalker had come to Phyrexia to stop him, accused him of lying about phthisis to destroy his enemies, and ordered him to surrender. He had shown her what he had done to the delegates, and while she stood horrified he stabbed her in the skull with a powerstone blade that scrambled her mind and kept her from planeswalking away. His priests were cutting her open, searching for the organ that let her walk between worlds, so that he could have it for himself.",
                        "Rebbec killed the priests, pulled out the blade and let Dyfed die. Then she went home to Halcyon. The card {Phyrexian Vivisector} remembers that the Father of Machines began as a twisted Thran physician, and that his legacy of medical atrocities lived on long after him in [New Phyrexia](praetors).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sealing-koilos", "Sealing Koilos",
                    listOf(
                        "Rebbec came back to a city that was dying. The Null Sphere's own artificers had turned on Yawgmoth. Rather than let him use it to shield Halcyon while he poisoned the armies outside, they sabotaged the station and gave their lives doing it, launching the Sphere up into the sky. It would hang there for five thousand years as the Null Moon, gathering white mana, until the skyship Weatherlight came for it in the last hours of the [Apocalypse](apocalypse). When Yawgmoth's stonechargers fell, nothing stopped the cloud. It killed the besieging armies, and then it rolled into Halcyon.",
                        "The people of Halcyon died in the streets or fled through the portal into Phyrexia, where they would become the first true Phyrexians. Rebbec found Glacian dead, and found the stones Yawgmoth had planted in him. Something of her husband had passed into them. His essence was trapped in the crystal, and for the first time in years he could think clearly. He told her what to do.",
                        "Rebbec carried the stone to the pedestal where Dyfed had first split it and set it in place. The portal closed. Yawgmoth, who had meant to wait out the cloud in Phyrexia and then come home, was locked out with his followers, and the seal would hold for five thousand years. Then Rebbec climbed the stairs of Halcyon into the white cloud and was gone.",
                        "The rest of the empire died on standing orders, its cities bombed by Yawgmoth's war caravels, and only scattered descendants of the Thran survived in remote corners of the world. The forces unleashed at Halcyon left behind a silent witness, the avatar printed as {Evra, Halcyon Witness}. Under the ruins the stone sat on its pedestal in the dark, holding Phyrexia shut, until two boys and their teacher came looking for Thran relics at [Koilos](koilos).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Light from the Null Moon took form—a mirage made real, alone in grandeur, isolated in a world that once had been its own.",
                        "Fall of the Thran, on Evra, Halcyon Witness",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Dominaria"),
                StorySource("Set", "Commander Legends"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Thran, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Thran-Phyrexian War"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dyfed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Rebbec"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Glacian"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "yawgmoths-spheres", "Yawgmoth's Spheres",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "nine-spheres", "Nine Spheres",
                    listOf(
                        "Phyrexia was a world somebody had built. Its maker was an ancient planeswalker, a humanoid who liked to wear the shape of a dragon, and almost nothing else is known about him except that he died about a month before [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) arrived. He left behind nine hollow spheres nested one inside another, a world of metal and machinery with a strange mechanical ecology of its own. When Yawgmoth first saw the First Sphere it was a sunlit paradise of artificial forests and plains, with mechanical serpents coiled around the trees. Under his rule it became a jungle dripping oily rain beneath a red, sunless sky.",
                        "Each sphere had its purpose. The Second was scrap and ruin under air that tasted of acid. The Third was a labyrinth of pipes whose distortions made it impossible to planeswalk any deeper. The Fourth held most of Phyrexia's people, and the vats where new Phyrexians were grown and remade, the sphere named on the card {The Fourth Sphere}. The Fifth was the Boiling Sea, an ocean of the glistening oil that ran through everything. The Sixth housed the Inner Circle, Yawgmoth's demons and highest servants, among fields of barbed wire under a solid white sky. The Seventh was an eternal furnace that powered the plane and punished those who failed it. The Eighth was pure energy. The Ninth was the control center of the world, and Yawgmoth's seat.",
                        "Dominarians would come to call it the Machine Hell, or the Nine Hells. Phyrexians came to believe it had always been his, and their scriptures say that Phyrexia evolved from the void and that Yawgmoth saw its perfection. They also knew that an artificial world would not last forever. Their survival, and their purpose, lay in the world he had been driven from.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Ash is our air, darkness our flesh.", "Phyrexian Scriptures, on Lurking Evil"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "grand-evolution", "The Grand Evolution",
                    listOf(
                        "Yawgmoth gave Phyrexia a purpose, to grow beyond its own spheres into the rest of the multiverse, and a creed to go with it. Flesh was weakness, prone to sickness, doubt and decay. Machines made by Yawgmoth and his priests were sacred. Phyrexians called his work phyresis, the war on disease, death and weakness, and the endless search for greater strength they called the Grand Evolution. Artifacts made by mortal hands were abominations to them, and the artificers who made them were worse.",
                        "Every Phyrexian began life as a newt, an androgynous, hairless human shape grown in a vat of oil in the Fourth Sphere. Newts were raised, tested, and then compleated, rebuilt into whatever Phyrexia needed. Some became soldiers fitted with blades and venom. Some became plague carriers or monstrous war engines. The devout became priests, the cards' {Priest of Yawgmoth}, and the most powerful became demons, the creatures printed as {Yawgmoth Demon}, who made up most of the Inner Circle.",
                        "Over the centuries a vast social order grew up, a food chain with church rites, in which every Phyrexian was judged by its ability to survive its own world. Through rebuilt Thran portals in the Fourth Sphere, Phyrexia reached out to other planes, conquered them, and fed their peoples into the vats as raw material. Where it ruled a world directly it set a governor over it. The title used on [Rath](rath-made) was evincar, and it may have been used for Phyrexian rulers on other planes as well.",
                        "None of it could reach Dominaria. Rebbec's seal at [Koilos](koilos) held for five thousand years, and the Phyrexians waited and plotted and built for the day the way back would open.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "From void evolved Phyrexia. Great Yawgmoth, Father of Machines, saw its perfection. Thus the Grand Evolution began.",
                        "Phyrexian Scriptures, on Dark Ritual",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "gix", "Gix",
                    listOf(
                        "The day came when two young archaeologists disturbed the stone at Koilos and the portal flickered open. Yawgmoth sent Gix, by then a demon of the Inner Circle, through as a scout, and Gix spent the Brothers' War working both sides, a story told with [The War](the-war/phyrexian-spies). By its end he had made Mishra a Phyrexian. Then [Urza](who/urza) set off the Golgothian Sylex, and Gix fled back to Phyrexia with a handful of human followers.",
                        "Gix's next plan was sleeper agents, newts grown to look human and seeded among Dominarians as spies, the kind printed as {Sleeper Agent}. The first ones all looked exactly alike, and when people noticed dozens of identical strangers they killed every one they could find. Gix had a better batch grown, taller, varied and convincingly human. Just as they were ready, the delayed result of the Sylex Blast closed around Dominaria, the barrier called the Shard, and Phyrexia's portals could no longer reach it. Someone had to pay for that. Yawgmoth had Gix seized, flayed and cast down into the Seventh Sphere, to be tortured there for thousands of years.",
                        "One of the first batch had been forgotten. She had been grown in the Fane of Flesh in the Fourth Sphere, one of hundreds of identical, sexless newts named only for the places where they slept, and her word, xantcha, meant the clots of slag beneath the furnaces. Gix himself oversaw her cadre and gave her that word as a name, the {Xantcha, Sleeper Agent} of the cards, and she chose to be a woman in answer to his being a man. She also thought for herself, which on Phyrexia was a fatal flaw. Hints in her story suggest that Gix grew her partly from Mishra's flesh. When the launches stopped she was left waiting for an assignment that never came, and she watched the punishment of Gix with her own eyes.",
                        "Xantcha went on serving as a seeker of relics on other worlds, and quietly sabotaged every artifact she was sent to fetch. On one of those worlds she was caught and about to be executed when a man with jewels for eyes stepped out of nowhere and saved her.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "urzas-assault", "Urza's Assault",
                    listOf(
                        "Urza had been hunting Phyrexia for centuries without ever finding it. Locked out of Dominaria by the Shard, he wandered the planes searching for the creatures that had taken his brother, and when he rescued Xantcha she told him where they lived. He spent the next centuries building an eight-legged dragon engine modeled on his brother's old machines, and a suit to keep out the plane's poisonous air.",
                        "When Phyrexians returned to the world where he had found Xantcha, Urza followed them home. He planeswalked straight onto the surface of Phyrexia and tore into it, smashing through the First, Second, Third and Fourth Spheres and blasting a hole all the way down. While he did, Xantcha slipped into the Fane of Flesh, waded into the pool where the newts' life matrices were kept, and took back the one that sang to her, the device she called her heart.",
                        "Then Yawgmoth noticed. He was no planeswalker, but he had spent millennia gathering godlike power in his own world, and he touched Urza's mind. Urza froze in the cockpit of his broken engine. Xantcha climbed in and shook him until he came back to himself, and with the last of his strength he planeswalked the two of them away. Phyrexia repaired the damage. Urza did not. Yawgmoth's touch had seeded a madness that would haunt him for centuries, and the god of Phyrexia now knew his enemy.",
                        "The two of them fled from plane to plane with Phyrexians always behind them, until Urza was healed by the planeswalker Serra and Freyalise's [World Spell](ice-age/world-spell) broke the Shard and let them home. Gix was freed from the Seventh Sphere to try again on Dominaria, and Urza and Xantcha tracked him to the caves of Koilos, where it had all begun. Xantcha and a young man named Ratepe died there so that Urza could destroy him. Urza kept her heart, the {Heartstone} of her old life, and later set it in the chest of a silver golem named [Karn](who/karn), whose story belongs to [The Legacy](the-legacy). Yawgmoth, his spies spent and his best agent dead, turned to a different plan, a world made of flowstone called [Rath](rath-made).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Yawgmoth brushed Urza's mind, and Urza's world convulsed.", "Corrupt"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Urza's Saga"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Planeswalker, by Lynn Abbey"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Thran, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Phyrexia"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Gix"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Xantcha"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Urza's Assault on Phyrexia"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Evincar"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "rath-made", "Rath",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-hub", "The Hub",
                    listOf(
                        "Infiltration had failed. [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth) turned to invasion, and an invasion needed a place to keep an army where no planeswalker would find it. So he made one. Rath was an artificial plane hidden in a small pocket of reality attached to Dominaria itself. Its origin was never fully explained, but it is said to have been made at his order some time after the Shard fell.",
                        "At the heart of Rath was a vast machine called the Hub, and in the Hub was the Stronghold, a fortress spanning a volcanic chasm in a volcano three miles high, the place printed as {Volrath's Stronghold}. Its refinery filtered magma so finely that each tiny grain of it was remade into a machine that could, through magic, be shaped into almost anything and made to look like almost any material. This was flowstone. It looked like ordinary sandstone and moved like lava, and nearly all of Rath was made of it. The black, tarry leavings of the process were dumped into the Death Pits below. Even its art could shape itself, as {Flowstone Sculpture} suggests.",
                        "Flowstone did two jobs. It held Rath's planar boundaries apart so that the artificial world did not collapse in on itself, and it added mass, constantly, so that Rath grew. That was the plan. When Rath's mass matched Dominaria's, the two planes would overlay. Rath would be set down on top of Dominaria, and every Phyrexian soldier stored inside it would be on Dominarian soil in a single moment, with no portals to guard and no gates to close.",
                        "Until then Rath was a fiefdom, ruled from the Stronghold by an appointed governor called the evincar, whose chief power was command of flowstone itself. Rath had other residents, too, and none of them had chosen to come.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The seed of a world's evil.", "Volrath's Stronghold"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "stolen-peoples", "Stolen Peoples",
                    listOf(
                        "Rath's sky was a maelstrom. About a thousand feet above the ground lay the plane's barrier, a band of energy that rose and fell without warning and tore apart anything caught in it. As Rath grew, regions of it briefly overlapped with Dominaria, and whatever stood in those places was pulled across. That is how most of Rath's people came to be there.",
                        "The tribes of the Kor, the Vec and the Dal wandered its deserts and plains, divided among themselves into those who resisted the evincar and those who served him. A floating home of elves and merfolk was dragged in along with a stretch of sea, and when food ran short the two peoples went to war. The elves grew the vast mangrove forest of {Skyshroud Forest} over the water, and the merfolk sank into the dark swamps beneath it and turned savage, helped along by the evincar's experiments. Three more peoples, the Dauthi, the Soltari and the Thalakos, never arrived at all. They were trapped in the Shadows between the two worlds, able to see both and touch neither, and two of them went mad. The cards remember the third keeping its sanity through faith, as the {Soltari Priest}.",
                        "Much of that was the work of Rath's first rulers. Croag, a member of Yawgmoth's Inner Circle, oversaw the plane, and in a Dominarian village called Coracin he found a sick old elder named Davvol, exiled by his own people, who betrayed his town's vault to him. Croag took Davvol to Rath, had him partly compleated, and set him to building the plane, crushing its rebels and killing [Urza](who/urza). Davvol bred ever deadlier {Phyrexian Negator} hunters for the planeswalker, and ran planeshifting experiments that dragged pieces of Dominaria across, which is how the Shadow peoples came to be stranded. When Croag was badly wounded on Dominaria, Davvol declared himself the first evincar. Croag recovered, took everything Davvol knew with a {Grafted Skullcap}, and left him to die.",
                        "The evincar who mattered most came later. He was born on Dominaria, and his name was Vuel.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("It is our duty to endure.", "Eladamri, Lord of Leaves, on Skyshroud Elf"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "volrath", "Volrath",
                    listOf(
                        "Vuel was the son of Sidar Kondo, chief of a war clan in northern Jamuraa. When his father took in an orphan named Gerrard, Vuel despised the boy at first, and then the two became friends, close as brothers. Vuel's rite of passage was to climb a harsh plateau. It was doomed before it began, because a man named Starke had poisoned the ritual paints on his skin. Gerrard saved his life. Vuel never forgave him for it, because being saved meant being cast out of his clan as a failure, rejected even by his father.",
                        "Starke found the outcast and fed his pride and his hunger for revenge. Vuel gathered warriors from the surrounding tribes and attacked his own clan, and many of his people died, his father among them. In the chaos he shut down the silver golem [Karn](who/karn) and stole the artifacts of Urza's [Legacy](the-legacy) that Gerrard was meant to inherit. Then he passed through Starke's portal to Rath.",
                        "There he heard Yawgmoth's words of power and gave himself to Phyrexia. Vuel became Volrath, the new evincar of Rath, a shapeshifter who could remake flesh and bone at will and a master of flowstone. He was cunning, utterly ruthless and supremely sure of himself, and he had not forgotten Gerrard. The card {Volrath, the Shapestealer} remembers the evincar who could take any shape he needed.",
                        "Volrath ruled Rath for years while the plane swelled toward the overlay. He kept the Legacy artifacts, he kept Starke close, and he waited for his brother to come looking for them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("I know to whom I owe the most loyalty, and I see him in the mirror every day.", "Starke, on Starke of Rath"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-overlay", "The Overlay",
                    listOf(
                        "In time Volrath set his trap. He had the captain of the skyship Weatherlight, Sisay, kidnapped and carried to Rath, knowing Gerrard would come after her. Gerrard came, and the raid on the Stronghold that followed is the crew's own story, told in the Weatherlight Saga's [Rath](rath-stronghold).",
                        "For Rath, what mattered was what came after. Volrath escaped disguised among the crew, failed to destroy them on [Mercadia](mercadia), and limped back to Rath to find that Phyrexia had declared his throne abdicated. Three rivals were fighting over it, among them Crovax, a nobleman of the Weatherlight's own crew who had fallen to Phyrexia, and Ertai, its young mage. Volrath challenged Crovax to a duel to the death and was winning when he suddenly collapsed. Ertai had tipped the fight. Crovax became evincar, the man printed as {Crovax the Cursed}.",
                        "At the center of Crovax's coronation was an execution. Stripped of his Phyrexian implants, Volrath was only Vuel again. He was injected with liquid flowstone, and Ertai worked it through his bloodstream until it took him apart from the inside. The last thing Vuel saw was the sky of his homeland, Dominaria, as the overlay began.",
                        "The Rathi Overlay was the second wave of Phyrexia's invasion. Rath came down on Dominaria and ceased to exist as a plane of its own, and its armies poured out across the world, a war told in [Invasion](invasion). The Stronghold became part of the island of Urborg. Deposits of flowstone lay scattered across the land afterward, and a new kind of mage learned to draw power from them. And over Skyshroud, which had come down in a cold corner of Dominaria, the overlay left a wound in time that would not close for centuries, one of the [rifts](time-rifts) that nearly ended the world.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
